  "cues": [
    {
      "id": "title",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 9.615,
      "text": "Since January 2026, steel entering the EU carries a carbon price. But the proof of how clean it is still travels by email, in spreadsheets."
    },
    {
      "id": "problem",
      "start": 9.965,
      "end": 23.812,
      "text": "Without verified data, the importer pays the EU default: 2.43 tonnes of CO₂ per tonne of Turkish hot-rolled steel, plus a mark-up. Yet 70% of Turkish steel comes from electric arc furnaces, far cleaner than that."
    },
    {
      "id": "landing",
      "start": 24.162,
      "end": 34.162,
      "text": "A clean mill is priced like a dirty one, so there's no reward for cutting emissions. And nothing stops the same low-carbon tonnes being sold twice."
    },
    {
      "id": "producer-evidence",
      "start": 39.12,
      "end": 59.505,
      "text": "Here's Anatolia Green Steel, a fictional electric-arc mill in İzmir. Its compliance officer loads the month's gas and electricity invoices, production report and meter log. The AI extraction step pulls out every quantity and links each one to the page it came from. She confirms each value. The AI never writes anything on-chain."
    },
    {
      "id": "producer-calc",
      "start": 62.48,
      "end": 92.095,
      "text": "The mill's 1,300 tonnes of pig iron come with their own verified passport from the supplier, so the calculator reads that 1.70 straight from the chain instead of the 1.85 default. Those tonnes are burned when this batch is verified, so no other product can claim them. The result: 0.337 tonnes of CO₂ per tonne of steel. Our anomaly check compares it with the plant's own 12-month history and flags one value for the verifier to review."
    },
    {
      "id": "producer-submit",
      "start": 92.445,
      "end": 100.138,
      "text": "Submitting commits only a salted Merkle root and a report hash to the chain. Competitors see nothing about the process."
    },
    {
      "id": "verifier",
      "start": 101.44,
      "end": 117.978,
      "text": "An accredited verifier, registered on-chain, checks the evidence against those commitments, reviews the flag, and signs twice: with an Ethereum key, and with ML-DSA, a NIST post-quantum signature, because these records must stay valid for years. The contract then mints 10,000 verified tonnes."
    },
    {
      "id": "shipments",
      "start": 121.24,
      "end": 127.394,
      "text": "The mill ships 6,000 tonnes to an importer in Hamburg, and 4,000 to one in Rotterdam."
    },
    {
      "id": "overship",
      "start": 141.2,
      "end": 150.046,
      "text": "Now watch: if it tries to sell 2,000 more 'verified' tonnes, the smart contract rejects it. Double counting isn't discouraged here. It's impossible."
    },
    {
      "id": "importer",
      "start": 152.52,
      "end": 160.212,
      "text": "When the Hamburg importer files its CBAM declaration, it surrenders those tonnes. They're burned, so they can never be reused."
    },
    {
      "id": "passport-pq",
      "start": 164.36,
      "end": 178.591,
      "text": "Anyone can open the passport from the QR code on the shipping documents. It shows who verified the batch, checks the post-quantum signature using chain data alone, and traces the pig iron back to its own passport."
    },
    {
      "id": "passport-cost",
      "start": 178.941,
      "end": 201.248,
      "text": "The pig iron in this steel came with its own verified passport. Those 1,300 tonnes were consumed on-chain, so no other mill can ever claim them. And against the EU default for Turkish hot-rolled steel, this one month's output saves about 47,000 euros this year, rising to 2.3 million euros a year when CBAM is fully phased in."
    },
    {
      "id": "passport-tamper",
      "start": 201.598,
      "end": 209.675,
      "text": "Here's the full trail, block by block. The original invoice matches. Change a single figure in it, and the check fails."
    },
    {
      "id": "why",
      "start": 215.44,
      "end": 221.978,
      "text": "Why blockchain? Producer, verifier and importer are in different countries, and the core risk is a double-spend."
    },
    {
      "id": "architecture",
      "start": 223.4,
      "end": 231.477,
      "text": "Under the hood: AI with a human in the loop, a post-quantum verifier signature, and a contract that conserves every tonne."
    },
    {
      "id": "outro",
      "start": 231.827,
      "end": 243.75,
      "text": "Next: pilots with exporters in Türkiye and India, the same data feeding Türkiye's ETS and India's carbon market, then cement and fertiliser. CarbonPass, for the Carbon Markets and Emissions Transparency track."
    }
  ],
